# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the synui test suite.
#
# Wired in from meson.build's add_test_setup(), so EVERY test gets it — not
# just the two shell scripts that used to export LSAN_OPTIONS themselves. The
# C-binary tests got no LSAN_OPTIONS at all before that, which is the whole
# reason cat_render failed on the libfontconfig/libcairo entries directly
# below: they were written, but never loaded for that test.
#
# One-time static caches inside system libraries — allocated on first use,
# never released by design, and not reachable from synui's own teardown.
leak:libfontconfig
leak:libcairo

# Mesa's software GLES2 driver. The smoke test renders on llvmpipe (no GPU on a
# CI runner — see smoke.sh), and mesa's driver init allocates per-screen caches
# under driCreateNewScreen3 that it never frees; every one of these stacks is
# rooted in EGL/gallium driver setup, not in anything synui allocates.
#
# Deliberately NOT suppressing libscenefx here even though it appears in these
# stacks: it is only the *caller* (wlr_egl_create_with_drm_fd), and suppressing
# it would blind this suite to real leaks in our own scenefx fork, which is
# exactly what the ASan run exists to catch.
leak:libEGL_mesa
leak:libgallium

# KNOWN REAL LEAK, upstream scenefx 0.4.1 — not ours, and bounded.
# fx_effect_framebuffers.c:addon_handle_destroy() frees the fx_effect_framebuffers
# struct but never wlr_buffer_drop()s the three buffers it owns
# (blur_saved_pixels_buffer, effects_buffer, effects_buffer_swapped), which
# fx_framebuffer_get_or_create_custom() allocated via wlr_allocator_create_buffer.
# That is exactly the 3 allocations / 624 bytes seen here.
#
# It leaks once per output AT SHUTDOWN and does not grow with uptime, so it is
# suppressed rather than patched: the file is from the upstream tarball, not one
# of the TUs our fork ports, so fixing it means carrying a patch in
# scenefx/PKGBUILD and rebuilding the package. Worth doing — see the note in the
# session summary — but not something to bury in a smoke-test change.
leak:fx_effect_framebuffers
leak:fx_framebuffer_get_or_create_custom

# NVIDIA's proprietary EGL/GBM driver, on any box with an NVIDIA GPU. This is
# the "1322 bytes leaked in 4 allocations" that failed smoke and resize_heal.
#
# It reads as a libdbus-1 leak and it is NOT one. libdbus is only the
# allocator: at EGL init the NVIDIA driver opens its own D-Bus connection
# (dbus_bus_register -> dbus_connection_send_with_reply_and_block) and keeps it
# for the life of the process, so the reply messages the loader queued are
# still held at exit. Three of the four allocations are those messages; the
# fourth is the NVIDIA GBM backend's per-device state under gbm_create_device.
#
# Proof it is the driver and not us: synui already destroys the allocator,
# renderer and backend in synui_destroy() (synui_main.c), so eglTerminate has
# run by the time LSan looks — and re-running the exact same binary with the
# NVIDIA vendor libraries out of the picture reports zero leaks and exits 0:
#
#   __EGL_VENDOR_LIBRARY_FILENAMES=/usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d/50_mesa.json \
#   LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 GBM_BACKEND=dri WLR_RENDERER_ALLOW_SOFTWARE=1 \
#   ./synui
#
# Scoped to the driver, deliberately NOT to libdbus-1. A blanket
# `leak:libdbus-1` would suppress by allocator rather than by owner — it would
# cover any future D-Bus use of our own, and it still would not catch the
# gbm_create_device allocation, which never touches libdbus at all.
#
# These only match with fast_unwind_on_malloc=0 (set alongside this file in
# meson.build): under the default frame-pointer unwinder the stacks truncate
# inside libdbus, and the GBM one truncates at "<unknown module>" on frame #1,
# with nothing attributable left to match on.
leak:libEGL_nvidia
leak:gbm_create_device
